.TH UALARM 3  "August 26, 1988"
.UC 2
.SH NAME
ualarm \- schedule signal after specified time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B unsigned ualarm(value, interval)
.B unsigned value;
.B unsigned interval;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.ft B
This is a simplified interface to setitimer(2).
.ft R
.PP
.B ualarm
causes signal SIGALRM, see
.IR signal (3),
to be sent to the invoking process
in a number of microseconds given by the
.I value
argument.
Unless caught or ignored, the signal terminates the process.
.PP
If the
.I interval
argument is non-zero, the SIGALRM signal will be sent
to the process every
.I interval
microseconds after the timer expires (e.g. after
.I value
microseconds have passed).
.PP
Because of scheduling delays,
resumption of execution of when the signal is
caught may be delayed an arbitrary amount.
The longest specifiable delay time is 65535 microseconds which is about 3
ticks of a 50 or 60Hz clock.
.PP
The return value is the amount of time previously remaining in the alarm clock.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
getitimer(2), setitimer(2), signal(3), sleep(3), alarm(3), usleep(3)
.SH NOTES
On the PDP-11,
.IR setitimer (2)
rounds the number of microseconds up to seconds resolution, therefore
.B ualarm
gives only 1 second of resolution.
